const V8_PACK={"version":8,"revision":"beginner-cues-v8","margins":0.3,"minimumBodyPt":6.1,"sheets":[[["notation","summary","percentile"],["discrete","discrete-cdf","loaded","events"],["density","piecewise"]],[["endpoint","uniform"],["normal","quantile","continuous-graph"],["sampling","linear","precision","binary","r-normal"]],[["one-t","pooled-t","t-output"],["anova","bonferroni"],["bonferroni-pairs","holm-t","holm","tables","anova-output"]],[["z-yates","dioxin","gof"],["chi2","large-chi"],["risk","folate-risk","odds","design","chi-output"]]],"blocks":{"notation":{"title":"TYPE OF DATA → CLASSIFY + SYMBOLS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Classify data or identify symbols. <b>BME423 · V8.</b> Population = entire group; sample = measured subset. Parameters are fixed; sample statistics vary across samples. Keep full calculator precision.</p><p>\\(x_i\\) (observation \\(i\\)); \\(n\\) (sample size); \\(\\bar x\\) (sample mean); \\(\\mu\\) (population mean); \\(s\\) (sample SD); \\(\\sigma\\) (population SD); \\(s^2,\\sigma^2\\) (variances). \\(\\sum\\) means add the specified terms.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> Interval–continuous: values within a range (BP, height, membrane potential). Interval–discrete: counts/rounded values (joints, texts, age in years). Categorical–nominal: unordered labels (country, ZIP). Categorical–ordered: ranked levels (cancer stage, severity). <b>TRAP</b> Numeric labels such as ZIP are not interval measurements.</p><p>\\(\\nu\\) (degrees of freedom); \\(\\alpha\\) (test significance level); \\(f\\) (mass/density); \\(F\\) (CDF).</p>","sources":["session2.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["data-scales","notation"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: additional symbol meanings","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"summary":{"title":"RAW LIST → MEAN, VARIANCE, SD, SEM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A raw list; asks for center, spread or error in its estimated mean. <b>WORKED</b> Heights (inches): 65, 69, 59, 64, 62, 67, 68, 62, 64, 65. Find mean, variance, SD and SEM.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Add the observations; divide by their count</h4>\\[\\bar x=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i}{n}\\]\\[n=10,\\quad \\sum x_i=645\\]\\[\\bar x=\\frac{645}{10}=64.5\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Subtract the mean, then square each difference</h4>\\[d_i=x_i-\\bar x,\\qquad d_i^2=(x_i-\\bar x)^2\\]\\[d_1=65-64.5=.5,\\quad d_1^2=.5^2=.25\\]\\[d_2=69-64.5=4.5,\\quad d_2^2=4.5^2=20.25\\]\\[d_3=59-64.5=-5.5,\\quad d_3^2=(-5.5)^2=30.25\\]\\[d_4=64-64.5=-.5,\\quad d_4^2=(-.5)^2=.25\\]\\[d_5=62-64.5=-2.5,\\quad d_5^2=(-2.5)^2=6.25\\]\\[d_6=67-64.5=2.5,\\quad d_6^2=2.5^2=6.25\\]\\[d_7=68-64.5=3.5,\\quad d_7^2=3.5^2=12.25\\]\\[d_8=62-64.5=-2.5,\\quad d_8^2=(-2.5)^2=6.25\\]\\[d_9=64-64.5=-.5,\\quad d_9^2=(-.5)^2=.25\\]\\[d_{10}=65-64.5=.5,\\quad d_{10}^2=.5^2=.25\\]\\[\\sum d_i^2=82.5\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Divide by one less than the sample size</h4>\\[s^2=\\frac{\\sum (x_i-\\bar x)^2}{n-1}\\]\\[s^2=\\frac{82.5}{10-1}=9.16667\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Square-root variance; scale SD for a mean</h4>\\[s=\\sqrt{s^2},\\qquad SEM=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}\\]\\[s=\\sqrt{9.16667}=3.02765\\]\\[SEM=\\frac{3.02765}{\\sqrt{10}}=.957427\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Estimated mean 64.5 inches; individual SD 3.02765 inches; mean-estimation error .957427 inch. <b>TRAP</b> SD describes individuals; SEM describes sample means. Variance has squared units.</p></div><p><b>TRAP</b> A complete population uses \\(N\\) and its population mean; a sample variance divides by \\(n-1\\).</p>\\[\\mu=\\frac{\\sum_i x_i}{N},\\qquad\\sigma^2=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2}{N}\\]<div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Squared-sum check for the same variance numerator</h4>\\[\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2=\\sum_i x_i^2-\\frac{(\\sum_i x_i)^2}{n}\\]\\[\\sum_i x_i^2=41685,\\qquad (\\sum_i x_i)^2=645^2=416025\\]\\[41685-\\frac{416025}{10}=41685-41602.5=82.5\\]<p>The same 82.5 is divided by 9 for the sample variance.</p></div><p><b>Estimators:</b> \\(\\bar x\\) estimates \\(\\mu\\); \\(s\\) estimates \\(\\sigma\\). Unbiased = centered correctly across repeated samples. For Normal populations, the sample mean has the minimum-variance property taught in class.</p>\\[\\widehat{SE}(s^2)=s^2\\sqrt{\\frac2{n-1}}=9.16667\\sqrt{\\frac29}=4.32121\\]<p>This estimates uncertainty in the sample variance for Normal observations; it is different from SEM.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session2.pdf"],"topics":["mean","variance","sd","sem"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: squared-sum shortcut and intermediate arithmetic","V5: estimator properties, uncertainty in variance, evaluated for the same height sample","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"percentile":{"title":"SORTED DATA → PERCENTILES, MEDIAN, IQR","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A data list; asks for a percentile, median, IQR or range. <b>WORKED</b> Sorted heights: 59, 62, 62, 64, 64, 65, 65, 67, 68, 69. Find the quartiles and spread.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Locate a percentile between sorted ranks</h4>\\[R=1+\\frac{p(n-1)}{100}=I+d\\]\\[R_{25}=1+\\frac{25(10-1)}{100}=3.25\\]\\[R_{50}=1+\\frac{50(10-1)}{100}=5.5\\]\\[R_{75}=1+\\frac{75(10-1)}{100}=7.75\\]<p>\\(p\\) (percentile number); \\(I\\) (integer rank); \\(d\\) (fraction of the next gap). <b>TRAP</b> Rank locates a value; interpolate the measurements, not their ranks.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Interpolate the measured values, not the ranks</h4>\\[x_p=x_{(I)}+d[x_{(I+1)}-x_{(I)}]\\]\\[Q_1=62+.25(64-62)=62.5\\]\\[Q_2=64+.5(65-64)=64.5\\]\\[Q_3=65+.75(67-65)=66.5\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. IQR and observed range (59–69)</h4>\\[IQR=Q_3-Q_1,\\quad \\text{range}=x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}\\]\\[IQR=66.5-62.5=4\\]\\[\\text{range}=69-59=10\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> Median 64.5; Q1 62.5; Q3 66.5; IQR 4; range 59–69. A straight Normal Q–Q plot supports Normal shape. Box = Q1–Q3 with median; usual whisker cutoff = 1.5×IQR; histogram = bin counts.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session2.pdf"],"topics":["percentiles","median","iqr"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: graphical summaries and Normal shape","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"discrete":{"title":"VALUES + PROBABILITIES → MEAN + VARIANCE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Possible values and their probabilities. <b>WORKED</b> Check validity; find mean, variance, CDF and probability from 2 inclusive to 7 exclusive.</p><table><thead><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>-1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(p(x)\\)</td><td>.55</td><td>.15</td><td>.10</td><td>.20</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Check both probability requirements</h4>\\[0\\le p(x)\\le1,\\qquad \\sum_xp(x)=1\\]\\[.55+.15+.10+.20=1\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Weight each value by its probability</h4>\\[\\mu=E[X]=\\sum_x x\\,p(x)\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}\\mu&=(-1)(.55)+2(.15)\\\\&\\quad+3(.10)+7(.20)\\\\&=-.55+.30+.30+1.40=1.45\\end{aligned}\\]<p>\\(X\\) (random variable); \\(x\\) (value); \\(p(x)\\) (its probability). <b>TRAP</b> Weight values by probability; average them equally only for equal probabilities.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Square the values before weighting</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\sum_x x^2p(x)\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}E[X^2]&=1(.55)+4(.15)\\\\&\\quad+9(.10)+49(.20)\\\\&=.55+.60+.90+9.80=11.85\\end{aligned}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Direct variance: weight EVERY squared deviation</h4>\\[v_x=(x-\\mu)^2p(x),\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sum_x v_x\\]\\[v_{-1}=(-1-1.45)^2(.55)=3.30138\\]\\[v_2=(2-1.45)^2(.15)=.045375\\]\\[v_3=(3-1.45)^2(.10)=.24025\\]\\[v_7=(7-1.45)^2(.20)=6.1605\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=3.30138+.045375+.24025+6.1605\\]\\[=9.7475\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Check with the second-moment shortcut</h4>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=11.85-1.45^2=9.7475\\]\\[SD(X)=\\sqrt{9.7475}=3.1221\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>6. Add only the included masses</h4>\\[P(X\\in A)=\\sum_{x\\in A}p(x)\\]\\[P(2\\le X<7)=p(2)+p(3)=.15+.10=.25\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> Mean 1.45; variance 9.7475; requested probability .25.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session3.pdf"],"topics":["pmf","expectation","variance-discrete"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"discrete-cdf":{"title":"AT MOST / AT LEAST → DISCRETE CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> “At most,” “at least,” or a CDF graph.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Accumulate probabilities through the endpoint</h4>\\[F(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\sum_{t\\le x}p(t)\\]\\[F(x)=\\begin{cases}0&x<-1\\\\.55&-1\\le x<2\\\\.70&2\\le x<3\\\\.80&3\\le x<7\\\\1&x\\ge7\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Include or exclude the endpoint mass</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a)\\]\\[P(X>2)=1-.70=.30\\]\\[P(X\\ge2)=1-P(X<2)=1-.55=.45\\]\\[P(-1<X<3)=p(2)=.15\\]</div><img class=\"v8-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-discrete.svg\" alt=\"Discrete mass plot and right-continuous step CDF\"><p><b>WRITE / SKETCH</b> Mass: separate spikes. CDF: jump by each mass; flat between values. <b>TRAP</b> Fill each jump’s upper point; open lower point. A discrete point can have positive probability.</p>","sources":["session3.pdf","hw1.pdf"],"topics":["cdf-discrete","graph-discrete"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"loaded":{"title":"LOADED DIE → USE THE STATED MASSES","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A die has unequal stated probabilities. <b>WORKED</b> \\(p(1)=.20\\); each of 2–6 has .16. Find \\(P(X\\le3)\\), mean and variance.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Weight using the stated masses</h4>\\[P(X\\le a)=\\sum_{x\\le a}p(x),\\quad E[X]=\\sum xp(x)\\]\\[P(X\\le3)=.20+.16+.16=.52\\]\\[E[X]=1(.20)+(2+3+4+5+6)(.16)=3.4\\]\\[E[X^2]=1(.20)+(4+9+16+25+36)(.16)=14.6\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=14.6-3.4^2=3.04\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> Probability .52; mean 3.4; variance 3.04. <b>TRAP</b> A loaded die does not give every face probability 1/6.</p>","sources":["session3.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["loaded-die"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"events":{"title":"SUCCESSIVE EVENTS → UPDATE WHAT REMAINS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Successive draws; check whether outcomes are replaced. <b>WORKED</b> Draw two cards from 52 without replacement. Find the chance both are kings.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Without replacement: update the second draw</h4>\\[P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B\\mid A)\\]\\[P(\\text{two kings})=\\frac4{52}\\frac3{51}=.00452489\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Without replacement, update numerator and denominator; independent probabilities stay unchanged. <b>WRITE</b> Two kings: .00452489. Three fair flips: exactly two heads = HHT/HTH/THH, 3 of 8 equally likely sequences.</p></div>","sources":["session3.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["conditional-probability"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"density":{"title":"CONTINUOUS DENSITY → INTEGRATE THE AREA","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A density formula over an interval; asks for areas or moments. <b>WORKED</b> \\(f(x)=x/2\\) on (0,2), zero elsewhere. Find area, CDF, mean, variance, tail and median.</p><p><b>Integration rule:</b> \\(dx\\) names the integration variable. Raise its power by one, divide by that new power; evaluate upper bound minus lower bound.</p>\\[\\int_a^b kx^r\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{kx^{r+1}}{r+1}\\right]_a^b\\quad(r\\ne-1)\\]<div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Check total area and nonnegative height</h4>\\[\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\,dx=1\\]\\[\\int_0^2\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\frac{2^2}{4}-\\frac{0^2}{4}=1\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Density height is not probability; area is probability. The density must be nonnegative. \\(\\int_a^b g(x)\\,dx=G(b)-G(a)\\) means upper-bound value minus lower-bound value.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Accumulate area only up to the requested value</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[F(x)=\\int_0^x\\frac t2\\,dt=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{4}\\right]_0^x=\\frac{x^2}{4}\\]\\[F(x)=\\begin{cases}0&x\\le0\\\\x^2/4&0<x<2\\\\1&x\\ge2\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Multiply by the outcome before integrating</h4>\\[\\mu=E[X]=\\int x f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^2 x\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\int_0^2\\frac{x^2}{2}\\,dx\\]\\[=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right]_0^2=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{6}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\frac{2^3}{6}-\\frac{0^3}{6}=\\frac86=\\frac43\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Use a second moment for variance</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\int x^2f(x)\\,dx,\\quad \\sigma^2=E[X^2]-\\mu^2\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\frac12\\int_0^2x^3\\,dx=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{8}\\right]_0^2=\\frac{2^4}{8}-\\frac{0^4}{8}=2\\]\\[\\sigma^2=2-\\left(\\frac43\\right)^2=\\frac{18-16}{9}=\\frac29\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. A right tail is the unaccumulated area</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a)\\]\\[F(1.5)=\\frac{1.5^2}{4}=.5625\\]\\[P(X>1.5)=1-.5625=.4375\\]\\[=\\int_{1.5}^2\\frac x2\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_{1.5}^2=\\frac44-\\frac{2.25}{4}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>6. A median splits the total area in half</h4>\\[F(m)=.5\\]\\[\\frac{m^2}{4}=.5\\Rightarrow m^2=2\\Rightarrow m=\\sqrt2\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Mean 4/3, variance 2/9, median \\(\\sqrt2\\). <b>TRAP</b> Keep the root inside the support. Continuous points have probability zero; inclusive endpoints do not change an area.</p></div>","sources":["session4.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["density","integration","cdf-continuous","moments","median-continuous"],"page":1,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: power rule, dx, constant factors, explicit powers and both bounds","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"piecewise":{"title":"DENSITY CHANGES FORMULA → PIECEWISE CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> The density uses different formulas in different intervals. <b>WORKED</b> \\(f(x)=x\\) on (0,1), \\(2-x\\) on [1,2). Find its CDF and \\(P(.5<X<1.5)\\).</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Carry the completed first area into the second piece</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[0<x<1:\\quad F(x)=\\int_0^x t\\,dt=\\frac{x^2}{2}\\]\\[1\\le x<2:\\quad F(x)=\\frac12+\\int_1^x(2-t)\\,dt\\]\\[=\\frac12+\\left[2t-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_1^x\\]\\[=\\frac12+\\left(2x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)-\\left(2-\\frac12\\right)\\]\\[=2x-\\frac{x^2}{2}-1\\]\\[F(x)=0\\ (x\\le0),\\qquad F(x)=1\\ (x\\ge2)\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Subtract accumulated areas</h4>\\[P(a<X<b)=F(b)-F(a)\\]\\[F(.5)=\\frac{.5^2}{2}=.125\\]\\[F(1.5)=2(1.5)-\\frac{1.5^2}{2}-1=.875\\]\\[P(.5<X<1.5)=.875-.125=.75\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> The requested interval probability is .75. <b>TRAP</b> Carry earlier area into the next piece; do not restart the CDF at zero. The CDF stays continuous at 1.</p></div>","sources":["session4.pdf"],"topics":["piecewise-density","piecewise-cdf"],"page":1,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: carry earlier area and subtract the lower bound explicitly","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"endpoint":{"title":"UNKNOWN ENDPOINT → TOTAL AREA ONE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Unknown support endpoint; the total density area must be one. <b>WORKED</b> \\(f(x)=.1+x\\) on \\(0<x\\le c\\), zero elsewhere. Find \\(c\\), CDF, moments and probabilities.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Force the full support area to equal one</h4>\\[\\int_0^c f(x)\\,dx=1\\]\\[\\int_0^c(.1+x)\\,dx=\\left[.1x+\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^c=1\\]\\[.1c+\\frac{c^2}{2}=1\\]\\[2(.1c)+2\\left(\\frac{c^2}{2}\\right)=2(1)\\]\\[.2c+c^2=2\\Rightarrow c^2+.2c-2=0\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Solve the quadratic; keep the valid endpoint</h4>\\[Ac^2+Bc+D=0:\\quad c=\\frac{-B\\pm\\sqrt{B^2-4AD}}{2A}\\]\\[c=\\frac{-.2\\pm\\sqrt{.2^2-4(1)(-2)}}{2}\\]\\[=\\frac{-.2\\pm\\sqrt{8.04}}{2}\\]\\[\\sqrt{8.04}=2.83549,\\quad c=\\frac{-.2+2.83549}{2}=1.31774\\]<p>\\(A=1,B=.2,D=-2\\). <b>TRAP</b> Use the root inside the support; the negative root \\(c_-=-1.51774\\) is invalid here. <b>WRITE</b> Upper endpoint \\(c=1.31774\\).</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Integrate only to the requested value</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[F(x)=\\left[.1t+\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_0^x=.1x+\\frac{x^2}{2}\\]\\[F(x)=0\\ (x\\le0),\\qquad F(x)=1\\ (x\\ge c)\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Multiply by x and expand before integrating</h4>\\[E[X]=\\int_0^c x f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[x(.1+x)=.1x+x^2\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^c(.1x+x^2)\\,dx\\]\\[=\\left[.05x^2+\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^c\\]\\[=.05(1.31774)^2+\\frac{(1.31774)^3}{3}\\]\\[= .849556\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Square x, not the entire density</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^c x^2f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[x^2(.1+x)=.1x^2+x^3\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{30}+\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_0^c\\]\\[=\\frac{(1.31774)^3}{30}+\\frac{(1.31774)^4}{4}\\]\\[=.830089\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>6. Subtract squared mean from second moment</h4>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=.830089-(.849556)^2\\]\\[=.108344\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>7. Use the CDF and complement</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a),\\quad P(X<b)=F(b)\\]\\[F(.5)=.1(.5)+\\frac{.5^2}{2}=.05+.125=.175\\]\\[P(X>.5)=1-F(.5)=1-.175=.825\\]\\[P(X<.6)=F(.6)=.1(.6)+\\frac{.6^2}{2}=.06+.18=.24\\]\\[P(X=.5)=0\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>8. Transform the mean without a new density</h4>\\[E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b\\]\\[E[2X+1]=2(.849556)+1=2.69911\\]</div>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session4.pdf"],"topics":["unknown-support","quadratic","continuous-expectation","continuous-variance"],"page":2,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: complete quadratic rearrangement, coefficient labels, root choice, boundary arithmetic","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"uniform":{"title":"CONSTANT DENSITY → UNIFORM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Constant density across a fixed range. <b>WORKED</b> For Uniform(0,1), find height, probability below .5, mean and variance.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Spread total area one across the interval</h4>\\[f(x)=\\frac1{b-a},\\quad P(u<X<v)=\\frac{v-u}{b-a}\\]\\[f(x)=\\frac1{1-0}=1,\\qquad P(0<X<.5)=\\frac{.5-0}{1-0}=.5\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^1x\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^1=\\frac12\\]\\[E[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}=\\frac{0+1}{2}=.5\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^1x^2\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1=\\frac13\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=\\frac13-\\frac14=\\frac{4-3}{12}\\]\\[=\\frac1{12}=\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Unknown coefficient: factor it out of the area</h4>\\[f(x)=kx\\ (0<x<2),\\quad k\\int_0^2x\\,dx=1\\]\\[k\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^2=1\\Rightarrow 2k=1\\Rightarrow k=\\frac12\\]</div><p><b>WRITE</b> Mean .5; variance 1/12. <b>TRAP</b> Normalize over the entire support. A CDF is zero before its support and one after it.</p>","sources":["session4.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["uniform","unknown-coefficient"],"page":2,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: integrate both moments and subtract squared mean","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"normal":{"title":"NORMAL CUTOFFS → LEFT / RIGHT / BETWEEN","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Normal mean and SD plus measurement cutoffs; asks for probability. <b>WORKED</b> Glucose \\(X\\sim N(95,8.7^2)\\). Find the areas below 85, between 85 and 115, and above 115.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Write the Normal density and accumulated area</h4>\\[f_X(x)=\\frac1{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right]\\]\\[f_X(x)=\\frac1{8.7\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(x-95)^2}{2(8.7)^2}\\right]\\]\\[F_X(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x} f_X(t)\\,dt\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> The second Normal parameter is variance; 8.7 is SD here. Standardize and use the table; the Normal CDF integral need not be solved by hand.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Convert measurement cutoffs to SD units</h4>\\[z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\]\\[z_{85}=\\frac{85-95}{8.7}=-1.14943\\approx-1.15\\]\\[z_{115}=\\frac{115-95}{8.7}=2.29885\\approx2.30\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Read accumulated LEFT area in the Normal table</h4>\\[\\Phi(z)=P(Z\\le z),\\quad \\Phi(-z)=1-\\Phi(z)\\]\\[\\Phi(1.15)=.8749\\Rightarrow\\Phi(-1.15)=.1251\\]\\[\\Phi(2.30)=.9893\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> The supplied table repeats complete z / LEFT-area pairs. Find 1.15 → .8749; 2.30 → .9893. For negative z, subtract the positive-z area from one.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Use the CDF for each requested region</h4>\\[P(X<a)=\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[P(X<85)=\\Phi(-1.15)=.1251\\]\\[P(a<X<b)=\\Phi(z_b)-\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[P(85<X<115)=.9893-.1251=.8642\\]\\[P(X>b)=1-\\Phi(z_b)\\]\\[P(X>115)=1-.9893=.0107\\]</div><p><b>TI-84:</b> DISTR → normalcdf(lower,upper,95,8.7). Use −1E99/1E99 for infinite bounds. Calculator values are .12519, .864053 and .0107567; small table-rounding differences are expected.</p>\\[z_0=\\frac{0-95}{8.7}=-10.9195\\]\\[P(X<0)=\\Phi(-10.9195)\\approx0,\\quad P(X=95)=0\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Table probabilities: .1251, .8642, .0107; they sum to one. An exact continuous point has zero area.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf","tables/z_table_NormalDist.pdf"],"topics":["normal","normal-tails","normal-table"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: correct lookup for the actual supplied paired-column z table","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"quantile":{"title":"GIVEN NORMAL AREA → CUTOFF / PERCENTILE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Given a Normal percentile, top-tail fraction or middle range. <b>WORKED</b> Find glucose Q1, Q3 and the central 95% interval.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Match cumulative area to a standard cutoff</h4>\\[\\Phi(z_p)=p,\\qquad x_p=\\mu+z_p\\sigma\\]\\[z_{.25}=-.67449,\\quad z_{.75}=+.67449\\]\\[x_{25}=95+(-.67449)(8.7)=89.1319\\]\\[x_{75}=95+(.67449)(8.7)=100.868\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Put half the omitted area in each tail</h4>\\[P(-z_*<Z<z_*)=.95\\Rightarrow\\Phi(z_*)=.975\\]\\[z_*=1.96\\]\\[x_L=\\mu-z_*\\sigma=95-1.96(8.7)=77.948\\]\\[x_U=x_{97.5}=\\mu+z_*\\sigma=95+1.96(8.7)=112.052\\]<p><b>TABLE / TI-84</b> invNorm(left area,95,8.7). Cumulative .975 gives z = 1.96. <b>TRAP</b> Right .025 means left .975; enter .975, not 97.5.</p></div><img class=\"v8-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-normal.svg\" alt=\"Normal density and cumulative probability with shaded intervals\"><p><b>WRITE</b> Q1 89.1319; Q3 100.868; central 95% 77.948–112.052 mg/dL. <b>SKETCH</b> Bell centered at \\(\\mu\\); shade the requested area. Normal CDF rises smoothly 0→1, crosses .5 at \\(\\mu\\), and has no jumps. Larger SD widens the bell.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf"],"topics":["inverse-normal","normal-graph"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"continuous-graph":{"title":"ASKED TO DRAW → CONTINUOUS PDF + CDF","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Asked to draw a continuous density and its accumulated probability.</p><img class=\"v8-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-continuous.svg\" alt=\"Homework density .1 plus x and its continuous cumulative function\"><p><b>WRITE / PDF</b> \\(f(x)=.1+x\\): zero outside (0,\\(c\\)]; rising line inside, from .1 to .1+\\(c\\). Shade intervals underneath. <b>TRAP</b> \\(f(.5)=.6\\) is a height, not probability.</p><p><b>WRITE / CDF</b> Zero before 0; \\(F(x)=.1x+x^2/2\\) inside; flat at 1 after \\(c\\). It is continuous and increasing; its slope is density height. Formula changes can create corners, not point-mass jumps.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session4.pdf"],"topics":["continuous-graph"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"sampling":{"title":"AVERAGE OF n → SAMPLING DISTRIBUTION / SEM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Probability concerns an average of n observations. <b>WORKED</b> Individuals have mean 4 and SD 1.5; independent n=50. Find the chance the sample mean is 3.5–3.8.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Keep the center; reduce variance for averaging</h4>\\[E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\]\\[E[\\bar X]=4,\\quad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{1.5^2}{50}=.045\\]\\[SD(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}=\\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{50}}=.212132\\]<p>\\(\\bar X\\) (random sample mean); \\(\\bar x\\) (one observed mean). If individuals are Normal, means are Normal at any sample size. Otherwise the CLT gives an approximately Normal mean for sufficiently large independent samples (course rule: \\(n\\ge30\\)).</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Divide each mean cutoff by the MEAN spread</h4>\\[z=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\]\\[z_L=\\frac{3.5-4}{.212132}=-2.35702\\]\\[z_U=\\frac{3.8-4}{.212132}=-.942809\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Subtract the accumulated areas</h4>\\[P(a<\\bar X<b)=\\Phi(z_b)-\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[\\Phi(-.942809)=.172889\\]\\[\\Phi(-2.35702)=.00921106\\]\\[P(3.5<\\bar X<3.8)=.172889-.00921106\\]\\[=.163678\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Round z to −.94 and −2.36; .1736−.0091=.1645. <b>WRITE</b> About 16.4% of sample averages fall in the interval. <b>TRAP</b> Use SEM for means; use \\(\\sigma\\) for an individual, without dividing by \\(\\sqrt n\\).</p></div>","sources":["session10.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["sampling","clt","sem-probability"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"linear":{"title":"SHIFT / SCALE / SUM → MEAN + VARIANCE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A constant is added, a variable is scaled, or independent variables are combined. <b>WORKED</b> For the glucose variable, find \\(E[X-26]\\) and its variance.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Apply coefficients directly to the mean</h4>\\[E[aX+b]=a\\mu+b,\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\sigma^2\\]\\[E[X-26]=95-26=69\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X-26)=1^2(8.7^2)=75.69\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. For independent variables, ADD variances</h4>\\[E[aX+bY]=a\\mu_X+b\\mu_Y\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY)=a^2\\sigma_X^2+b^2\\sigma_Y^2\\]<p>For two independent measurements with the glucose distribution:</p></div>\\[E[X+Y]=95+95=190\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)=8.7^2+8.7^2=151.38\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)=8.7^2+(-1)^2(8.7^2)=151.38\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> A shift changes the mean, not spread. <b>TRAP</b> Square coefficients for variance; even subtraction adds independent variances. \\(X+X=2X\\) is not an independent sum; its variance is \\(4\\sigma^2\\).</p>\\[E[h(X)]=\\sum_xh(x)p(x)\\quad\\text{(discrete)}\\]\\[E[h(X)]=\\int h(x)f(x)\\,dx\\quad\\text{(continuous)}\\]","sources":["session3.pdf","session10.pdf"],"topics":["linear-transform","independent-sum"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: general expectation of a function","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"precision":{"title":"LARGER SAMPLE → SMALLER SEM","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Asked how changing sample size changes error in the estimated mean. <b>WORKED</b> Height SD 3.02765, SEM .957427, n=10. Find SEM for four times as many observations.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Compare sample sizes with individual spread held fixed</h4>\\[SEM_{\\rm new}=SEM_{\\rm old}\\sqrt{\\frac{n_{\\rm old}}{n_{\\rm new}}}\\]\\[n_{\\rm new}=4(10)=40\\]\\[SEM_{\\rm new}=.957427\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{40}}=.478714\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Four times n halves SEM to .478714. For target SEM, \\(n=(s/SEM_{\\rm target})^2\\); round upward. <b>TRAP</b> A more precise mean does not require smaller individual variation.</p></div>","sources":["session10.pdf"],"topics":["precision","sample-size"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: estimator properties and uncertainty in sample variance","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"one-t":{"title":"ONE SAMPLE vs FIXED MEAN → t TEST","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One numerical sample versus a fixed reference mean. <b>WORKED</b> USC students: n=16, mean LDL 103, SD 16 mg/dL; reference 115. Independent Normal responses: is the student population mean different at .05?</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the population hypotheses</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0,\\qquad H_A:\\mu\\ne\\mu_0\\]\\[H_0:\\mu=115,\\qquad H_A:\\mu\\ne115\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the undergraduate population mean equals 115 under the null; it differs from 115 under the alternative. \\(\\mu_0\\) is the fixed reference, not another sample mean.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Estimate the mean’s standard error</h4>\\[SE=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}\\]\\[SE=\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\frac{16}{4}=4\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Standardize the observed departure</h4>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt n}\\]\\[t=\\frac{103-115}{4}=\\frac{-12}{4}=-3\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Use the one-sample degrees of freedom</h4>\\[\\nu=n-1,\\qquad |t|>t_{\\rm crit}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]\\[\\nu=16-1=15\\]\\[t_{\\rm crit}=t_{.025,15}=2.13145\\]\\[|t|=3>2.13145\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> t row 15, total two-tail .05 → 2.131. <b>WRITE</b> At .05, student population mean LDL differs from 115 mg/dL; the observed mean is lower.</p></div><p><b>p-value</b> \\(p=2P(T_{15}\\ge3)=.00897274\\). <b>TRAP</b> The reference is fixed, not another sample. Compare p with .05; p is not the probability the null is true.</p>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session8.pdf"],"topics":["one-sample-t","hypothesis-words"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"pooled-t":{"title":"TWO NUMERICAL GROUPS → POOLED t","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two independent numerical groups; common variance, possibly unequal n. <b>WORKED</b> Control n=22, mean 49, SD 8.4; intervention n=27, mean 57, SD 9.5. Normal populations: do their means differ at .05?</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the hypotheses for the two populations</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\mu_2,\\qquad H_A:\\mu_1\\ne\\mu_2\\]<p><b>In words:</b> control and intervention have equal population means under the null; their population means differ under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Pool VARIANCES, weighted by their df</h4>\\[s_p^2=\\frac{(n_1-1)s_1^2+(n_2-1)s_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}\\]\\[s_1^2=8.4^2=70.56,\\qquad s_2^2=9.5^2=90.25\\]\\[s_p^2=\\frac{21(70.56)+26(90.25)}{22+27-2}\\]\\[=\\frac{1481.76+2346.50}{47}=81.4523\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Compute the standard error of the difference</h4>\\[SE_{\\rm diff}=\\sqrt{s_p^2\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_{\\rm diff}=\\sqrt{81.4523\\left(\\frac1{22}+\\frac1{27}\\right)}\\]\\[=2.59213\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Divide the observed mean gap by its SE</h4>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x_1-\\bar x_2}{SE_{\\rm diff}}\\]\\[t=\\frac{49-57}{2.59213}=-3.08627\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Compare with a two-sided cutoff</h4>\\[\\nu=n_1+n_2-2,\\quad |t|>t_{\\rm crit}\\]\\[\\nu=22+27-2=47\\]\\[|t|=3.08627>t_{.025,47}=2.01174\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> df 47 lies between rows 46/48; total two-tail .05 cutoffs 2.013/2.011. The statistic exceeds both. <b>WRITE</b> Population means differ at .05; intervention’s observed mean is higher (p=.00339345). <b>TRAP</b> Weight variances by df when n differs; do not just average them.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session8.pdf"],"topics":["pooled-t","unequal-sample-sizes"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"t-output":{"title":"SUPPLIED t OUTPUT → READ df + p","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> A printed t-test result; asks for the test, df, p or conclusion.</p><pre>Welch Two Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 15.611\np-value = 0.0003625\nmean of x   mean of y\n64.5        69.7</pre><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, population mean heights differ; the first observed mean is lower. Read \\(t\\) (statistic), df and p. <b>TRAP</b> Welch can have fractional df: use the printed df, not pooled df.</p><pre>Two Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 18\np-value = 0.0002594</pre><p><b>TABLE / METHOD</b> “Two Sample t-test” uses pooled \\(\\nu=n_1+n_2-2\\). The hand method assumes common variance; follow the supplied test name.</p>","sources":["session8.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["t-output"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"anova":{"title":"EQUAL-SIZE NUMERICAL GROUPS → ANOVA","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Several numerical groups of equal size; asks whether any population mean differs. <b>WORKED</b> Filgrastim: n=15 per dose; neutrophils in \\(10^9\\) cells/L. Test all-equal means at .05, then compare pairs.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Dose</th><th>Mean</th><th>SD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low</td><td>3.7</td><td>1.1</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>4.6</td><td>1.4</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>5.2</td><td>2.1</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the overall population hypotheses</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\mu_2=\\mu_3\\]\\[H_A:\\text{not all population means are equal}\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the three dose population means are equal under the null; at least one differs under the alternative. Assume independent Normal responses and equal population variances.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Average the group variances for within spread</h4>\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^m s_j^2}{m}\\]\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=\\frac{1.1^2+1.4^2+2.1^2}{3}\\]\\[=\\frac{1.21+1.96+4.41}{3}=2.52667\\]<p>\\(m=3\\) (number of groups); \\(n=15\\) (people per group); \\(s_j\\) (SD in group \\(j\\)).</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Average the means; square their deviations</h4>\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=\\frac{\\sum_j\\bar x_j}{m}\\]\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=\\frac{3.7+4.6+5.2}{3}=4.5\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_j(\\bar x_j-\\bar{\\bar x})^2&=(3.7-4.5)^2\\\\&\\quad+(4.6-4.5)^2+(5.2-4.5)^2\\\\&=.64+.01+.49=1.14\\end{aligned}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Divide by m−1, then multiply by group size</h4>\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{\\sum_j(\\bar x_j-\\bar{\\bar x})^2}{m-1},\\quad s_{\\rm bet}^2=ns_{\\bar x}^2\\]\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{1.14}{3-1}=.57\\]\\[s_{\\rm bet}^2=15(.57)=8.55\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Divide between by within</h4>\\[F=\\frac{s_{\\rm bet}^2}{s_{\\rm wit}^2}\\]\\[F=\\frac{8.55}{2.52667}=3.38391\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>6. Use numerator and denominator df in that order</h4>\\[\\nu_1=m-1,\\quad\\nu_2=m(n-1)\\]\\[\\nu_1=3-1=2,\\quad\\nu_2=3(15-1)=42\\]\\[F=3.38391>F_{.05;2,42}=3.21994\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> F: numerator df 2 across top, denominator df 42 down side; upper .05 lightface → 3.22. <b>WRITE</b> At .05, at least one dose population mean differs (p=.0433947). <b>TRAP</b> Use variances, not SDs; the overall test does not identify the pair.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session6.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["balanced-anova","anova-variance","anova-df"],"page":3,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"anova-output":{"title":"SUPPLIED ANOVA → READ F + WITHIN VARIANCE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Printed ANOVA output; asks for an overall decision or follow-up pairs.</p><pre class=\"aov-output\">            Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(&gt;F)  \nGroup        2  779.4   389.7   5.832 0.0106 *\nResiduals   19 1269.5    66.8                 </pre><p><b>Read:</b> Group df 2 = F numerator df; Residuals df 19 = denominator and pairwise df. Residuals Mean Sq 66.8 = within variance; F value 5.832 = test statistic; Pr(&gt;F) .0106 = p-value.</p>\\[F=\\frac{MS_{\\rm Group}}{MS_{\\rm Residual}}=5.832\\quad\\text{(unrounded inputs)}\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, at least one population mean differs; at .01, a difference is not established. Retain residual variance and df for requested pairs, using their actual sample sizes.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>If residual STANDARD ERROR is supplied, square it</h4>\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=(\\text{residual standard error})^2\\]\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=(8.17407)^2=66.8154\\approx66.8\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Residual standard error is an SD: square it. Residual Mean Sq is already a variance.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session7.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["anova-output","assumptions"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: full summary(aov) fields, alpha comparison and residual-SD conversion","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"bonferroni":{"title":"WHICH MEAN PAIRS? → BONFERRONI","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Named mean pairs; asks for comparisons at one family significance level. <b>WORKED</b> Compare the filgrastim dose pairs at family \\(\\alpha=.05\\).</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the hypothesis for EACH dose pair</h4>\\[H_{0,ij}:\\mu_i=\\mu_j,\\quad H_{A,ij}:\\mu_i\\ne\\mu_j\\]<p><b>In words:</b> each null says the two named dose populations have equal means; each alternative says those population means differ.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Divide family alpha by the requested pair count</h4>\\[k=\\begin{cases}m(m-1)/2&\\text{all pairs}\\\\m-1&\\text{vs control}\\end{cases}\\]\\[\\alpha_{\\rm pair}=\\frac{\\alpha_{\\rm family}}{k}\\]\\[k=\\frac{3(3-1)}2=3,\\quad\\alpha_{\\rm pair}=\\frac{.05}{3}=.0166667\\]\\[\\nu=m(n-1)=3(14)=42\\]\\[t_{\\rm crit}=t_{\\alpha_{\\rm pair}/2,42}=2.49367\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Count the requested family, not every possible pair. Retain ANOVA within variance and residual df. Total two-tail .0166667 equals one-tail .00833333.</p></div><p><b>TABLE / TI-84</b> t row 42, total two-tail .0166667. Missing column: DISTR → invT; enter LEFT area and original df:</p>\\[t_{\\rm crit}=\\operatorname{invT}(1-\\alpha_{\\rm pair}/2,\\nu)\\]\\[=\\operatorname{invT}(1-(.05/3)/2,42)=2.49367\\]<div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Compute the shared SE using the original within variance</h4>\\[SE_{ij}=\\sqrt{s_{\\rm wit}^2\\left(\\frac1{n_i}+\\frac1{n_j}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_{ij}=\\sqrt{2.52667\\left(\\frac1{15}+\\frac1{15}\\right)}\\]\\[=\\sqrt{\\frac{2(2.52667)}{15}}=.580421\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Low versus Medium</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{LM}|=\\frac{|3.7-4.6|}{.580421}=1.5506\\]\\[1.5506<2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{do not reject }H_{0,LM}\\]</div><p><b>Remaining dose pairs continue at the top of the next column.</b></p>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["bonferroni","all-pairs","pairwise-equations"],"page":3,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: requested comparison family, all pairs versus control","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"holm":{"title":"PAIRS vs CONTROL → HOLM CHI-SQUARE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Printed chi-square pairs; asks which treatments differ from control. <b>WORKED</b> Mortality statistics: 18.2341, 9.49443, 4.68951; each df 1. Test the three control comparisons at family .05.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Order the evidence, then reduce the remaining family</h4>\\[\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\alpha}{k-j+1}\\]\\[k=3:\\quad\\alpha_1=.05/3=.0166667\\]\\[\\alpha_2=.05/2=.025,\\qquad\\alpha_3=.05/1=.05\\]<p>Order p smallest first; with common df, order \\(\\chi^2\\) largest first. <b>In words:</b> each null says treatment and control have equal mortality probabilities; its alternative says they differ.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Compare each ordered statistic with its own cutoff</h4>\\[\\chi_j^2>\\chi^2_{\\alpha_j,1}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_{0,j}\\]\\[18.2341>6.635>\\chi^2_{.0166667,1}\\quad\\text{reject}\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 1: .0166667 is absent; the stricter .01 cutoff 6.635 already proves the first rejection.</p>\\[9.49443>\\chi^2_{.025,1}=5.02389\\]\\[4.68951>\\chi^2_{.05,1}=3.84146\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> All three treatments differ from control at family .05. <b>TRAP</b> Only the three requested control pairs count; stop at the first failure and retain all remaining nulls.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["holm","chi-square-posthoc"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: handle the absent chi-square alpha column with a printed-table bound","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"tables":{"title":"CRITICAL VALUE / p RANGE → READ THE TABLE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Asked for a critical value, p-value range or missing table entry.</p><p><b>TABLE</b> z: complete z / adjacent LEFT area; negatives use symmetry. t: df row, full TWO-TAILED alpha (.05 means .05). F: numerator df across top, denominator down side; UPPER tail. Chi-square: df row, RIGHT tail; either supplied chi-square table works.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Bound a p-value between printed table columns</h4>\\[t_{\\rm two\\ tail\\ .10,4}<|t|<t_{\\rm two\\ tail\\ .05,4}\\]\\[2.132<2.58630<2.776\\Rightarrow .05<p_{\\rm two\\ sided}<.10\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Here .05 &lt; p &lt; .10. Missing df? Read neighboring rows: above both cutoffs → reject; below both → do not reject; between them → exact cutoff or supplied p needed.</p></div><p><b>TRAP / TI-84</b> Positive t cutoff \\(\\operatorname{invT}(1-\\alpha/2,df)\\); two-sided p \\(2\\operatorname{tcdf}(|t|,1E99,df)\\). Halve alpha for invT only, not again for the two-tail table.</p>","sources":["session15.pdf","session9.pdf","tables/t_table.pdf","tables/F_table.pdf","tables/Chi_Squared_Table1.pdf","tables/Chi_Squared_Table2.pdf"],"topics":["table-reading","tails"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: printed table tails, p-value bracketing, missing rows and TI-84 conversions","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"z-yates":{"title":"TWO PROPORTIONS → YATES z","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two groups; each person has event/no event. <b>WORKED</b> Thrombi: 18/25 placebo versus 6/19 aspirin. Are population event probabilities different at .05?</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the population hypotheses and sample fractions</h4>\\[H_0:\\pi_1=\\pi_2,\\quad H_A:\\pi_1\\ne\\pi_2\\]\\[\\hat p_1=\\frac{x_1}{n_1}=\\frac{18}{25}=.72\\]\\[\\hat p_2=\\frac{x_2}{n_2}=\\frac6{19}=.315789\\]<p><b>In words:</b> placebo and aspirin have equal thrombus probabilities under the null; the probabilities differ under the alternative. \\(x_i\\) is the event count; \\(n_i\\) is that group’s total.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Pool event counts for the null probability</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{x_1+x_2}{n_1+n_2}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac{18+6}{25+19}=\\frac{24}{44}=.545455\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Calculate the null SE and half-count adjustment</h4>\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{.545455(.454545)\\left(\\frac1{25}+\\frac1{19}\\right)}\\]\\[=.151547\\]\\[c_Y=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)\\]\\[c_Y=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{25}+\\frac1{19}\\right)=.0463158\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Shrink the absolute observed gap, then standardize</h4>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{\\max(0,|\\hat p_1-\\hat p_2|-c_Y)}{SE_0}\\]\\[D_Y=\\max(0,|.72-.315789|-.0463158)\\]\\[=.357895\\]\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{.357895}{.151547}=2.36161\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Make the two-sided decision</h4>\\[|z_Y|>z_{\\alpha/2}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]\\[2.36161>1.95996\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Normal cumulative .975, or t infinite-df row, total two-tail .05 → 1.960. <b>WRITE</b> Population thrombus probabilities differ at .05; aspirin’s observed rate is lower. <b>TRAP</b> Retain Yates for the course’s 2×2 comparisons.</p></div>","sources":["session11.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["two-proportions","yates-z"],"page":4,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic supplied chi-square output and corrected z/chi-square equivalence","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"chi2":{"title":"2×2 COUNTS → YATES CHI-SQUARE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Two groups × two outcomes; observed COUNTS. <b>WORKED</b> Test whether folate use and neural tube defects are associated at .05, using the table below.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Event</th><th>No event</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Folate</td><td>4</td><td>471</td><td>475</td></tr><tr><td>No folate</td><td>27</td><td>498</td><td>525</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>31</td><td>969</td><td>1000</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State independence in the population</h4>\\[H_0:\\text{group and outcome are independent}\\]\\[H_A:\\text{group and outcome are associated}\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the malformation probability is the same with and without folate under the null; it differs under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Build ALL expected counts from margins</h4>\\[E_{ij}=\\frac{R_iC_j}{N}\\]\\[E_{11}=\\frac{475(31)}{1000}=14.725\\]\\[E_{12}=\\frac{475(969)}{1000}=460.275\\]\\[E_{21}=\\frac{525(31)}{1000}=16.275\\]\\[E_{22}=\\frac{525(969)}{1000}=508.725\\]<p>\\(R_i\\) (row total); \\(C_j\\) (column total); \\(N\\) (grand total); \\(O_{ij}\\) (observed count).</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Calculate EVERY corrected cell contribution</h4>\\[c_{ij}=\\frac{\\max(0,|O_{ij}-E_{ij}|-.5)^2}{E_{ij}}\\]\\[c_{11}=\\frac{(|4-14.725|-.5)^2}{14.725}=7.10021\\]\\[c_{12}=\\frac{(|471-460.275|-.5)^2}{460.275}=.227148\\]\\[c_{21}=\\frac{(|27-16.275|-.5)^2}{16.275}=6.424\\]\\[c_{22}=\\frac{(|498-508.725|-.5)^2}{508.725}=.205515\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Add contributions; compare on the right tail</h4>\\[\\chi_Y^2=\\sum_i\\sum_j c_{ij},\\quad\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)\\]\\[\\chi_Y^2=7.10021+.227148\\]\\[\\qquad+6.424+.205515=13.9569\\]\\[\\nu=(2-1)(2-1)=1\\]\\[13.9569>\\chi^2_{.05,1}=3.84146\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 1, upper .05 → 3.841. <b>WRITE</b> Folate and neural tube defects are associated at .05 (p=.00018705). <b>TRAP</b> Use counts, not percentages; Yates shrinks the absolute gap before squaring.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session12.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-2x2","all-expected-cells","all-contributions"],"page":4,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"large-chi":{"title":"LARGER COUNT TABLE → ORDINARY CHI-SQUARE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> More than two rows or columns of observed counts. <b>WORKED</b> Depression event/no-event counts: (36,131), (41,132), (75,104). Compare the population probabilities at .05.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the hypotheses; total rows and columns</h4>\\[H_0:\\pi_1=\\pi_2=\\pi_3,\\quad H_A:\\text{not all }\\pi_i\\text{ equal}\\]\\[(R_1,R_2,R_3)=(167,173,179)\\]\\[(C_1,C_2)=(152,367),\\qquad N=519\\]<p><b>In words:</b> all three population event probabilities are equal under the null; at least one differs under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Calculate ALL six null expected counts</h4>\\[E_{ij}=\\frac{R_iC_j}{N}\\]\\[E_{11}=\\frac{167(152)}{519}=48.9094\\]\\[E_{12}=\\frac{167(367)}{519}=118.091\\]\\[E_{21}=\\frac{173(152)}{519}=50.6667\\]\\[E_{22}=\\frac{173(367)}{519}=122.333\\]\\[E_{31}=\\frac{179(152)}{519}=52.4239\\]\\[E_{32}=\\frac{179(367)}{519}=126.576\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Calculate ALL six contributions (no Yates)</h4>\\[c_{ij}=\\frac{(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\\]\\[c_{11}=\\frac{(36-48.9094)^2}{48.9094}=3.40739\\]\\[c_{12}=\\frac{(131-118.091)^2}{118.091}=1.41124\\]\\[c_{21}=\\frac{(41-50.6667)^2}{50.6667}=1.8443\\]\\[c_{22}=\\frac{(132-122.333)^2}{122.333}=.763851\\]\\[c_{31}=\\frac{(75-52.4239)^2}{52.4239}=9.7223\\]\\[c_{32}=\\frac{(104-126.576)^2}{126.576}=4.02667\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. Add and select the larger-table df</h4>\\[\\chi^2=\\sum c_{ij},\\quad\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)\\]\\[\\chi^2=3.40739+1.41124+1.8443\\]\\[\\quad+.763851+9.7223+4.02667\\]\\[=21.1757\\]\\[\\nu=(3-1)(2-1)=2\\]\\[21.1757>\\chi^2_{.05,2}=5.99146\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 2, upper .05 → 5.991. <b>WRITE</b> Population depression probabilities differ at .05. <b>TRAP</b> No Yates for 3×2; follow-up pairs identify which groups differ.</p></div><pre>X-squared = 21.176, df = 2\np-value = 2.522e-05</pre>\\[\\texttt{2.522e-05}=.00002522\\]","sources":["session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-larger","all-six-cells"],"page":4,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic larger-table output and scientific notation","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"gof":{"title":"ONE LIST vs TARGET % → GOODNESS OF FIT","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One observed category list versus stated target percentages. <b>WORKED</b> Counts 255, 125, 90; target probabilities .60, .25, .15. Test fit at .05.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. State the target distribution; find expected counts</h4>\\[H_0:(\\pi_1,\\pi_2,\\pi_3)=(.60,.25,.15)\\]\\[H_A:\\text{at least one population proportion differs}\\]\\[N=255+125+90=470,\\quad E_i=Np_i\\]\\[E_1=470(.60)=282\\]\\[E_2=470(.25)=117.5,\\quad E_3=470(.15)=70.5\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the population follows the specified target proportions under the null; it does not follow that distribution under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Work EVERY category, then sum</h4>\\[c_i=\\frac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i},\\quad\\chi^2=\\sum_i c_i\\]\\[c_1=\\frac{(255-282)^2}{282}=2.58511\\]\\[c_2=\\frac{(125-117.5)^2}{117.5}=.478723\\]\\[c_3=\\frac{(90-70.5)^2}{70.5}=5.39362\\]\\[\\chi^2=2.58511+.478723+5.39362\\]\\[=8.45745\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Use category count minus one</h4>\\[\\nu=k-1,\\quad\\chi^2>\\chi^2_{\\alpha,\\nu}\\]\\[\\nu=3-1=2,\\qquad8.45744681>5.99146\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Chi-square row 2, upper .05 → 5.991. <b>WRITE</b> Reject the target proportions at .05 (p=.01457). <b>TRAP</b> Use specified targets, not observed fractions; targets sum to one. No Yates.</p></div>","sources":["session12.pdf"],"topics":["goodness-of-fit"],"page":4,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic goodness-of-fit output","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"binary":{"title":"ONE EVENT COUNT → PROPORTION + SE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> One group; asks for the event proportion and its error. <b>WORKED</b> 8 events among 61 patients. Estimate the proportion and SE.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Use event count divided by all patients</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{x}{n},\\quad s_{\\rm binary}=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac8{61}=.131148\\]\\[s_{\\rm binary}=\\sqrt{.131148(.868852)}=.337562\\]\\[SE(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}n}\\]\\[SE(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{.131148(.868852)}{61}}=.0432203\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Estimated event proportion .131148; SE .0432203. <b>TRAP</b> SE describes uncertainty in the estimated proportion, not variation among individual zero/one outcomes.</p></div>","sources":["session11.pdf"],"topics":["binary-proportion","proportion-se"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"risk":{"title":"FOLLOWED GROUPS → RR, ARR, NNT / NNH","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Followed groups; asks for treatment benefit or harm. <b>WORKED</b> Aspirin 6/19 events; placebo 18/25. Find RR, ARR and NNT.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Find BOTH event risks from group totals</h4>\\[r_g=\\frac{\\text{events in group }g}{\\text{all people in group }g}\\]\\[r_T=\\frac6{19}=.315789\\]\\[r_C=\\frac{18}{25}=.72\\]<p>\\(T\\) (treatment); \\(C\\) (control). <b>TRAP</b> Risk divides events by ALL group members; odds divide events by nonevents.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Divide for relative risk; subtract for reduction</h4>\\[RR=\\frac{r_T}{r_C},\\qquad ARR=r_C-r_T\\]\\[RR=\\frac{6/19}{18/25}=.438596\\]\\[ARR=\\frac{18}{25}-\\frac6{19}=.404211\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Aspirin risk is .439 times placebo risk; absolute reduction 40.42 percentage points. <b>TRAP</b> Relative and absolute reductions differ.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Take the reciprocal of ABSOLUTE risk reduction</h4>\\[NNT=\\frac1{ARR}\\]\\[NNT=\\frac1{.404211}=2.47396\\Rightarrow3\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Treat 3 people per additional event prevented over the study interval. <b>TRAP</b> Round NNT upward; reciprocals use decimal risks.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>4. If treatment increases harm, use NNH</h4>\\[ARI=r_T-r_C,\\quad NNH=\\frac1{ARI}\\]\\[ARI=.08-.03=.05,\\qquad NNH=\\frac1{.05}=20\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Small baseline risks: relative benefit versus absolute benefit</h4>\\[RR=\\frac{r_T}{r_C},\\quad RRR=1-RR,\\quad ARR=r_C-r_T\\]\\[r_C=10/1000=.010,\\quad r_T=8/1000=.008\\]\\[RR=\\frac{.008}{.010}=.8,\\quad RRR=1-.8=.20\\]\\[ARR=.010-.008=.002,\\quad NNT=1/.002=500\\]\\[\\frac{NNT}{NNH}=\\frac{500}{20}=25\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Here 20% relative benefit is .2 percentage points absolute. With NNH 20, 25 additional harms accompany one additional prevented event.</p></div>","sources":["session13.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["risk","arr","nnt","nnh"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: low-baseline-risk NNT/NNH tradeoff","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"folate-risk":{"title":"RISK COMPARISON → NAME THE NUMERATOR","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Risk in one group relative to another.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Compare no-folate risk with folate risk</h4>\\[r_g=\\frac{x_g}{n_g},\\qquad RR_{A/B}=\\frac{r_A}{r_B}\\]\\[r_{\\rm no\\ folate}=\\frac{27}{525}=.0514286\\]\\[r_{\\rm folate}=\\frac4{475}=.00842105\\]\\[RR_{\\rm no/yes}=\\frac{27/525}{4/475}=6.10714\\]\\[RR_{\\rm yes/no}=\\frac{4/475}{27/525}=.163743\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> No-folate risk is 6.11 times folate risk. <b>TRAP</b> Reversing the ratio gives its reciprocal; name its direction.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session13.pdf"],"topics":["risk-orientation"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"odds":{"title":"CASES CHOSEN BY DISEASE → ODDS RATIO","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Selected by existing disease; compare earlier exposure. <b>WORKED</b> Cases: 50 exposed, 14 unexposed; controls: 43 exposed, 35 unexposed. Find the exposure OR.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Exposed</th><th>Unexposed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cases</td><td>50</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Controls</td><td>43</td><td>35</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>Divide exposed by unexposed in EACH selected group</h4>\\[OR=\\frac{a/b}{c/d}=\\frac{ad}{bc}\\]\\[O_{\\rm case}=\\frac{50}{14}=3.57143\\]\\[O_{\\rm control}=\\frac{43}{35}=1.22857\\]\\[OR=\\frac{50/14}{43/35}=\\frac{50(35)}{14(43)}\\]\\[=\\frac{1750}{602}=2.90698\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> Cases have 2.91 times controls’ exposure odds. <b>TRAP</b> Case–control sampling fixes case numbers; sample fractions cannot estimate population disease risks. Use OR.</p></div>","sources":["session13.pdf"],"topics":["odds-ratio","case-control"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"design":{"title":"ASSIGNED / SELECTED / FOLLOWED → DESIGN","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Selection, treatment assignment or follow-up.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> Randomized trial: assign treatment, follow outcomes. Cohort: follow exposure groups without assignment; both give risks/RR. Case–control: select by outcome, assess earlier exposure; OR. Parallel: different people; crossover: same people in different periods. Masking hides assignment from participants, clinicians/evaluators or statisticians. Sham mimics the procedure. <b>TRAP</b> Crossover needs stability/no carryover; case–control samples do not give disease risks.</p>","sources":["session10.pdf","session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["design","chi-square-output"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"]},"r-normal":{"title":"SUPPLIED NORMAL OUTPUT → AREA OR CUTOFF?","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Supplied Normal command/output; decide whether input is a cutoff or an area.</p><p><b>WRITE</b> pnorm: cutoff → area; qnorm: area → cutoff. Default LEFT; lower.tail=FALSE means RIGHT.</p><pre>pnorm(85,95,8.7)\n[1] 0.12519\npnorm(115,95,8.7,lower.tail=FALSE)\n[1] 0.0107567\nqnorm(.975,95,8.7)\n[1] 112.052</pre><p><b>TRAP</b> Right .025 equals left .975. Third argument is SD; use SEM for a sample average.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["normal-output","lower-tail"],"provenance":["V5: authentic Normal-output examples; interpretation only","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"],"page":2,"column":3},"holm-t":{"title":"ORDERED t PAIRS → HOLM; STOP AT FAILURE","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Supplied t pairs; asks which differ while controlling one family. <b>WORKED</b> Five G1 comparisons; df 46; ordered \\(|t|\\) = 3.3, 2.9, 2.3, 2.2, 2.1. Use family .05.</p>\\[H_{0,1j}:\\mu_1=\\mu_j,\\quad H_{A,1j}:\\mu_1\\ne\\mu_j\\]<p><b>In words:</b> each null says G1 and that named group have equal population means; its alternative says their means differ.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Order strongest evidence first; adjust the remaining family</h4>\\[\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\alpha_{\\rm family}}{k-j+1}\\]\\[\\alpha_1=\\frac{.05}{5}=.0100,\\quad\\alpha_2=\\frac{.05}{4}=.0125\\]\\[\\alpha_3=\\frac{.05}{3}=.0166667\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Order p smallest first; with common df, order \\(|t|\\) largest first. Stop at the first failure.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Check each ordered pair using its own two-sided cutoff</h4>\\[|t_j|>t_{\\alpha_j/2,\\nu}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject and continue}\\]\\[G1:G6:\\quad3.3>t_{.005,46}=2.687\\quad\\text{reject}\\]\\[G1:G3:\\quad2.9>t_{.00625,46}=2.59973\\quad\\text{reject}\\]\\[G1:G5:\\quad2.3<t_{.00833333,46}=2.48469\\quad\\text{STOP}\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> At family .05, G1 differs from G6 and G3. Retain G1:G5, G1:G4 and G1:G2 without further tests. <b>TABLE</b> t row 46, current two-tail alpha; if absent, invT(1−alpha/2,46).</p></div>","sources":["session9.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["holm-t","holm-stop"],"provenance":["V5: full ordered t example including the stopping decision","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"],"page":3,"column":3},"dioxin":{"title":"ROUNDED PROPORTIONS → POOL EXACT COUNTS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Rounded group proportions plus an exact combined event count. <b>WORKED</b> Female births after father-only dioxin: .565 of 186; mother-only: .455 of 220. Exactly 205/406 female overall. Compare population probabilities at .05.</p>\\[H_0:\\pi_F=\\pi_M,\\quad H_A:\\pi_F\\ne\\pi_M\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the two populations have equal female-birth probabilities under the null; they differ under the alternative.</p><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>1. Pool the exact combined count; keep the stated group fractions</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{\\text{total events}}{n_1+n_2}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac{205}{186+220}=\\frac{205}{406}=.504926\\]<p><b>TRAP</b> Pool the exact total event count; do not reconstruct group counts from rounded proportions.</p></div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>2. Find the null SE and half-count correction</h4>\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)(1/n_1+1/n_2)}\\]\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{.504926(.495074)(1/186+1/220)}\\]\\[=.0498017\\]\\[c_Y=\\tfrac12(1/n_1+1/n_2)\\]\\[c_Y=.5(1/186+1/220)=.0049609\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>3. Shrink the gap, divide by SE and compare</h4>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{\\max(0,|\\hat p_1-\\hat p_2|-c_Y)}{SE_0}\\]\\[D_Y=|.565-.455|-.0049609=.105039\\]\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{.105039}{.0498017}=2.10915>1.960\\]<p><b>TABLE</b> Normal cumulative .975 / t infinite-df two-tail .05 → 1.960. <b>WRITE</b> Female-birth probabilities differ at .05; father-exposed sample’s fraction is higher.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session11.pdf"],"topics":["rounded-proportions","dioxin"],"provenance":["V5: actual HW2 rounded-proportion case, rewritten with V6 operation labels","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"],"page":4,"column":1},"bonferroni-pairs":{"title":"BONFERRONI → REMAINING DOSE PAIRS","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Continue the same three-pair family with the original SE and cutoff.</p>\\[SE_{ij}=.580421,\\quad t_{\\rm crit}=2.49367\\]<div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>5. Low versus High</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{LH}|=\\frac{|3.7-5.2|}{.580421}=2.58433\\]\\[2.58433>2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_{0,LH}\\]</div><div class=\"v8-step\"><h4>6. Medium versus High</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{MH}|=\\frac{|4.6-5.2|}{.580421}=1.03373\\]\\[1.03373<2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{do not reject }H_{0,MH}\\]<p><b>WRITE</b> At family .05, only low versus high population means differ. <b>TRAP</b> Nonsignificant pairs do not establish identical means.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["all-pairs","pairwise-equations"],"provenance":["V6: complete Low–High and Medium–High substitutions, adjacent-column continuation","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"],"page":3,"column":3},"chi-output":{"title":"SUPPLIED CHI-SQUARE → READ STATISTIC + p","html":"<p><b>TRIGGER</b> Printed statistic, df or decision.</p><pre>Pearson&#x27;s Chi-squared test with\nYates&#x27; continuity correction\nX-squared = 5.5772, df = 1\np-value = 0.0182</pre><p><b>WRITE</b> At .05, aspirin/placebo probabilities differ: p=.0182 &lt; .05. X-squared = statistic; p-value = RIGHT-tail area. <b>TRAP</b> Yates is already included; do not correct again. Here \\(\\chi_Y^2=z_Y^2\\).</p>","sources":["session11.pdf","session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-output","z-chi-square-equivalence"],"provenance":["V5: authentic output, attached to the same clinical data as the risk example","V8: question-to-method heading; TRIGGER, WRITE, TRAP and applicable TABLE cues"],"page":4,"column":3}}};
